Check the gaps between furniture and walls. Cats can get stuck in narrow spaces. Common spots include:
• between cabinets and the wall;
• behind the refrigerator;
• behind the washing machine;
• between the sofa and the wall.

The simple rule: either the cat shouldn't be able to fit into a space at all, or it must be able to get back out.

Hide cords in cable covers. Kittens love to chew on cords, which can cause electric shock, death, or burns to the mouth.

Dangerous spots:
• extension cords;
• phone chargers;
• computer cables;
• string lights;
• TV wires.

Using dedicated cable covers to bundle wires together and conceal them really helps.

Keep in mind that cats may chew on cords out of curiosity, because of a smell, or in reaction to a person's behavior. If this happens, don't scare the cat or yell at it — calmly and gently distract it from the cords instead.

Anchor bookshelves and TVs to the wall. Cats are excellent jumpers, and their weight can tip over unsecured items, which can injure the cat or people nearby.

Main items to watch:
• bookcases;
• dressers;
• shelving units;
• TVs;
• wall shelves;
• wine cabinets.

Any household chemical is dangerous for animals. This includes products for:
• cleaning glass;
• cleaning the bathroom;
• washing floors;
• washing dishes, and so on.

Even if a chemical gets on a cat's fur, the cat will lick it off and it will end up in its internal organs. Keep all products away from your cat's living areas and behind closed cabinet doors.

Especially dangerous: bleach, drain cleaners, oven cleaner, dishwasher tablets, laundry pods, and concentrated cleaning solutions.

Cats are creatures of habit and security. Never place food near the litter box.
• A quiet corner free of drafts;
• Away from noisy appliances;
• A view of the room for a sense of safety.

• eyes — no discharge, excessive tearing, or redness;
• nose — no unusual discharge;
• ears — no strong odor, excessive wax, or constant scratching;
• teeth and gums — plaque, bad breath, redness;
• skin and coat — dandruff, scabs, hair loss, parasites, matting;
• paws and claws — cracks, damage, overly long claws;
• weight and body condition — sudden changes need attention;
• behavior — appetite, activity, sleep, litter box use.

If a cat is bored, it may:
• wake you up at night;
• scratch furniture;
• meow loudly;
• overeat;
• overgroom to the point of bald patches;
• attack your legs.

• Leaving a mug of coffee next to your laptop;
• using scented candles near the cat;
• keeping medication on the desk;
• leaving scissors open;
• storing thread and hair ties at your workspace.

• Complete and balanced food — the packaging should say "Complete"/"Complete and balanced" and be suited to the right life stage (kitten/adult/spayed-neutered/senior);
• The first ingredients are protein, named specifically ("chicken," "salmon," "turkey"), not vague terms like "meat";
• Fats are listed specifically (chicken fat, fish oil), not vaguely;
• No BHA, BHT, ethoxyquin, artificial colors, or flavor enhancers among the additives;
• Vitamins and minerals are present, and the guaranteed analysis meets AAFCO standards;
• The packaging is checked: verify the expiration date and that the seal is intact before buying.
